## 5. Made Several Tools Check Their Own Work Automatically
This is the big one — instead of just trusting the output, these tools now **catch their own mistakes** before showing you the file:

- **AR-15**: now scans the finished form for any leftover blank spots that should've been filled in.
*(Example: if a date field's fill-in code had a small bug and skipped it, before you'd only notice by manually reading all 15 pages. Now the tool itself says "hey, this field is still blank" automatically.)*

- **AR-9**: now does real math-checking, not just blank-checking.
*(Layman: it adds up all the individual expense items — telephone, electricity, rent, etc. — and checks that they actually equal the "Total A" figure written on the form. If someone's ₹500 off somewhere, it tells you exactly where instead of you re-adding 8 numbers by hand.)*
*(Example: if Telephone=3,869 + Electricity=18,999 + Rent=52,320 + others don't actually add up to the Total A written on the form, the tool now flags "Section A mismatch: components sum to X but total_a = Y" — a genuine calculator built into the process.)*

- **F-347 (Assets & Liabilities form)**: checks that Assets − Liabilities = Net Worth.
*(Layman: this is the same math a bank auditor would do by hand — the tool now does it instantly and flags it if the numbers don't balance, before the document ever reaches you.)*

- **Current Account Opening Form**: I found something interesting — this tool already HAD a built-in double-check function written into it, but nobody had actually connected it to run! It was just sitting there unused.
*(Layman: imagine you installed a smoke detector in your house but never actually put the battery in — it existed, but wasn't doing anything. I found the "battery" step was missing and switched it on.)*
*(Example: now it actually confirms the customer's name, mobile number, and Aadhaar number genuinely appear correctly in the finished form, not just that the filling process "ran without crashing.")*

- **Thanks-Letter Processor**: now checks every single extracted letter for missing details.
*(Layman: this tool reads old thank-you letters using OCR (scanning text from images) to build address labels. OCR sometimes misreads things. Now, if a name or mobile number comes back blank because the scan failed, the tool tells you exactly which letter (like "Letter #47") needs a manual look — instead of quietly printing a blank label.)*

- **Title Deed Register Boxes**: found and proved a real bug.
*(Layman: this tool shrinks text to fit inside a printed box, like fitting a paragraph onto a business card. I discovered that in extreme cases — if the text is REALLY long — the tool would shrink the font all the way down and just declare "done!" even though the text still didn't actually fit in the box.)*
*(Example: I tested this myself by feeding it a huge block of text on purpose. Before my fix, it would have printed something that overflowed the box when cut out. Now it correctly says "⚠ text overflows even at minimum font — must be shortened" instead of pretending everything's fine.)*

- **PA-36 (Stock Inspection Certificate)**: added ONLY the safe, uncontested part of checking — blank-field scanning and flagging accounts where the balance reads as zero (which usually means the scanner misread the PDF).
*(Layman: I added a basic safety net here too, but ONLY for things we agreed on. I did NOT touch the stock/insurance number calculation — more on that below.)*

